               memories across event boundaries",
  author    = "Griffiths, Benjamin J and Fuentemilla, Lluís",
  journal   = "Hippocampus",
  publisher = "Wiley",
  volume    =  30,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "162--171",
  abstract  = "Our lives are a continuous stream of experience. Our episodic
               memories on the other hand have a definitive beginning, middle,
               and end. Theories of event segmentation suggest that salient
               changes in our environment produce event boundaries which
               partition the past from the present and, as a result, produce
               discretized memories. However, event boundaries cannot completely
               discretize two memories; any shared conceptual link will lead to
               the rapid integration of these memories. Here, we present a new
               framework inspired by electrophysiological research that resolves
               this apparent contradiction. At its heart, the framework proposes
               that hippocampal theta-gamma coupling maintains a highly abstract
