                 valuation and action selection. In the SCG, transition
                 statistics accumulate in a dense predictive representation,
                 while nonlinear selection determines which transitions are
                 expressed as graph edges. Consequently, gradual strengthening
                 can trigger discrete reorganization of graph topology and
                 abrupt behavioral shifts. Across human reward and transition
                 revaluation tasks, the SCG explains bimodal and trimodal
                 behavioral regimes as emergent consequences of distinct graph
                 configurations. Across human and mouse two-step tasks, dynamic
                 graph reconfiguration captures canonical reward-by-transition
                 interactions without requiring mixtures of control systems. In
                 mice, transitions preceding reward strengthened more rapidly,
                 biasing graph topology toward reward-directed paths. Temporally
                 precise optogenetic dopamine stimulation produced behavioral
                 effects consistent with accelerated graph edge formation
                 predicted by the SCG. The model further generates a testable
                 prediction: graph topology determines the geometry of
                 low-dimensional population activity. Directed acyclic graphs
                 yield activity concentrated at graph entry and goal states,
                 whereas cyclic graphs produce periodic, grid-like structure.
